# Target C-II — a vanishing overswap removes the diameter-pair-minimality dependence from the TB1 slack fork

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` strengthening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the one-sign side of the strong-TB1 exact contact-swap fork through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** TB1 exact contact-swap obstruction fork; canonical contact-minimal normalization  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tb1_overswap_contact_minimality_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The exact contact-swap theorem used the second intersection of the two boundary unit-contact circles,

\[
v_{sw}=-(a+b)=-qw,
\qquad q=|a+b|=O(D^{-1}),
\]

and then invoked the secondary diameter-pair-minimal normalization if nonswitching old diameters remained.

That secondary normalization is unnecessary. Move an asymptotically negligible amount **past** the second intersection.

Put

\[
\lambda_D:=1+{1\over D},
\qquad
v_{out}:=-\lambda_D(a+b)=-\lambda_D q w.
\]

Then both boundary contact families become strictly longer than one, while every one-sign switching diameter remains strictly shorter than `D`. The displacement still has the same leading scale

\[
|v_{out}|
\le {31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\]

Therefore, if every previously strict source pair stayed strictly inside `(1,D)`, `v_out` would produce either a strict sub-`D` source or another diameter-`D` global minimizer with **strictly fewer unit contacts**. Both contradict the canonical normalization.

So every one-sign strong-TB1 interface already forces the same inverse-`D` inactive witness using contact minimality alone:

\[
1<|xy|\le1+{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1})
\]

or

\[
D-{31.4\times10^6\over D}-o(D^{-1})\le|xy|<D.
\]

The earlier contact-swap fork and inactive-slack matching consequences remain unchanged. This note strengthens their logical dependency: the near-active branch is forced before the secondary diameter-pair count is used.

---

## 1. Exact boundary-contact formula beyond the swap

Let `a,b` be the rooted unit boundary-contact vectors and define

\[
q:=|a+b|>0,
\qquad
w:={a+b\over q}.
\]

For any scalar `lambda`, translate the physical layer by

\[
v(\lambda):=-\lambda(a+b)=-\lambda q w.
\tag{OS.1}
\]

Since

\[
q^2=|a+b|^2=2(1+a\cdot b),
\]

a direct expansion gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
|a+v(\lambda)|^2
&=|a-\lambda(a+b)|^2\\
&=1-2\lambda(1+a\cdot b)+\lambda^2q^2\\
&=1+q^2\lambda(\lambda-1).
\end{aligned}
\tag{OS.2}
\]

The same identity holds for `b`:

\[
\boxed{
|a+v(\lambda)|^2
=|b+v(\lambda)|^2
=1+q^2\lambda(\lambda-1).
}
\tag{OS.3}
\]

Thus:

- `lambda=0` is the original contact configuration;
- `lambda=1` is the exact contact swap, with both lengths again exactly `1`;
- every `lambda>1` strictly opens both boundary contact families.

Take

\[
\boxed{\lambda_D=1+D^{-1}.}
\tag{OS.4}
\]

Then

\[
|a+v(\lambda_D)|^2-1
=q^2\left(1+{1\over D}\right){1\over D}>0,
\tag{OS.5}
\]

and likewise for `b`.

The opening can be arbitrarily small; only strict positivity is needed for contact minimality.

---

## 2. The overswap still shortens every one-sign moving diameter

At a one-sign strong-TB1 interface, every moving actual diameter has inward vector `n_h` satisfying

\[
n_h\cdot w\ge(c_H-o(1))D,
\qquad
c_H=0.453819885927\ldots .
\tag{OS.6}
\]

For a moving diameter, the exact adjacent-layer formula at `v(lambda)` is

\[
\begin{aligned}
|r_h(v(\lambda))|^2-D^2
&=2n_h\cdot v(\lambda)+|v(\lambda)|^2\\
&=-2\lambda q(n_h\cdot w)+\lambda^2q^2\\
&=\lambda q\bigl(-2n_h\cdot w+\lambda q\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\tag{OS.7}
\]

The TB1 cone collapse gives

\[
q<{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{OS.8}
\]

With `lambda=lambda_D=1+1/D`,

\[
\lambda_Dq=O(D^{-1}),
\]

whereas

\[
2n_h\cdot w\ge(2c_H-o(1))D.
\]

Therefore the bracket in (OS.7) is negative for all sufficiently large `D`, uniformly over every moving actual diameter at the interface. Hence

\[
\boxed{
|r_h(v(\lambda_D))|<D
\quad\text{for every moving old actual diameter.}
}
\tag{OS.9}
\]

Nonswitching source-pair vectors are unchanged exactly by the layer translation, as in the parent adjacent-strip theorem.

---

## 3. Contact minimality alone now forces a strict inactive witness

Assume, for contradiction, that after the overswap `v(lambda_D)` every previously strict source pair remains strictly feasible:

\[
|xy|>1\Longrightarrow |x(v)-y(v)|>1,
\tag{OS.10}
\]

and

\[
|xy|<D\Longrightarrow |x(v)-y(v)|<D.
\tag{OS.11}
\]

All old contacts away from the two boundary train families are unchanged exactly. Equations (OS.3)--(OS.5) show that **every** contact in the two boundary families is now strict. Assumption (OS.10) says no previously strict pair becomes a new unit contact. Therefore the translated source has strictly fewer unit contacts than the canonical source.

By (OS.9), every moving old actual diameter becomes strict. Every nonswitching old diameter is unchanged exactly. Assumption (OS.11) says no previously strict pair reaches or exceeds `D`.

Thus either:

1. some nonswitching old diameter remains, in which case the new source is another global minimizer of diameter `D` with strictly fewer unit contacts, contradicting contact minimality; or
2. no nonswitching old diameter remains, in which case the new source has diameter strictly below `D`, contradicting global minimality.

Therefore at least one previously strict pair must satisfy at the overswap endpoint

\[
|x(v)-y(v)|\le1
\quad\text{or}\quad
|x(v)-y(v)|\ge D.
\tag{OS.12}
\]

No diameter-pair count is used.

---

## 4. The witness scale is unchanged to leading order

The overswap displacement has norm

\[
|v(\lambda_D)|
=\lambda_Dq
=\left(1+{1\over D}\right)q.
\tag{OS.13}
\]

Using (OS.8),

\[
\boxed{
|v(\lambda_D)|
<{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{OS.14}

The extra factor `1+1/D` contributes only `O(D^-2)` to the displayed leading scale.

If a previously strict noncontact pair ends at distance at most `1`, the triangle inequality gives

\[
1<|xy|\le1+|v(\lambda_D)|.
\]

If a previously strict nondiameter pair ends at distance at least `D`, then

\[
D-|v(\lambda_D)|\le|xy|<D.
\]

Therefore the same two asymptotic witness windows follow:

\[
\boxed{
1<|xy|
\le1+{31.4\times10^6\over D}+o(D^{-1})
}
\tag{OS.15}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
D-{31.4\times10^6\over D}-o(D^{-1})
\le|xy|<D.
}
\tag{OS.16}
\]

All multiplicity and matching consequences from the parent contact-swap theorem therefore carry over verbatim.

---

## 5. Geometric interpretation

The two near-antipodal boundary contact constraints have two nearby exact intersection configurations in translation space. The original strong-TB1 blocker obstructs the local contact-opening branch at the first intersection. If all switching diameter vectors have the same transverse sign, they **do not** obstruct the contact-opening branch immediately beyond the second intersection: they all shorten there.

Thus a one-sign strong-TB1 layer can survive only because a constraint that was inactive in the original source lies within `O(D^-1)` of activation and blocks this second contact-opening sheet.

The resulting fork is source-level and finite:

```text
one-sign strong TB1
+
near-antipodal boundary contacts
->
second contact intersection at distance O(1/D)
->
vanishing overshoot strictly opens both old boundary train families
+
all old moving diameters strictly shorten
->
contact minimality forces an inactive pair within O(1/D) of 1 or D.
```

This is stronger than an infinitesimal tangent obstruction and stronger than the exact-swap argument's dependency bookkeeping. The remaining issue is the geometry of the inactive witnesses, not a secondary normalization artifact.

**Disposition:** replaced the exact-swap use of diameter-pair minimality by a `1+1/D` overswap that opens both boundary train families while still shortening all one-sign switching diameters. The same inverse-`D` inactive-slack fork now follows from contact minimality alone. C-II and Level I remain open.
